6:07.288Z"}, "id": 3, "domain": "VFD / Variable Frequency Drive Faults & Operation", "difficulty": "medium", "type": "scenario", "correct_answer": "A", "model_answer": "A", "parse_rule": "leading_letter", "is_correct": true, "http": 200, "turn_status": "answered", "basis": "general_reasoning", "trace_id": "6cb03652bd2edd42f1d79a40e213eb5d", "response_time_ms": 5440, "answer_text": "A  \n\n- Verify the drive’s input voltage (de‑energize, lock‑out/tag‑out the supply, then measure the line voltage at the drive’s input terminals).  \n- If the input is within the drive’s rated range, check that the DC bus is charging properly (measure DC bus voltage after isolation).  \n- Confirm the drive’s output voltage setting matches the commanded frequency (ensure the V/Hz profile is correct).  \n- Inspect the motor nameplate to ensure the motor is rated for the supply voltage you have.  \n\n*Is the measured input voltage at the drive within its specified nominal range?*"}
