69Z"}, "id": 24, "domain": "Motor Theory & Troubleshooting", "difficulty": "hard", "type": "scenario", "correct_answer": "D", "model_answer": "D", "parse_rule": "leading_letter", "is_correct": true, "http": 200, "turn_status": "answered", "basis": "general_reasoning", "trace_id": "e01e3ffa38234257dcd76409bc44fab1", "response_time_ms": 5569, "answer_text": "D – the T1‑T3 reading is high, indicating a problem with that leg (damaged lead or high‑resistance connection).  \n\n**Check list**  \n1. **Verify isolation** – lock‑out/tag‑out the motor and confirm no voltage is present before opening any terminals.  \n2. Visually inspect the T1‑T3 wiring and connector for corrosion, loose pins, or damaged insulation.  \n3. With the motor still isolated, measure each individual winding resistance (T1‑T2, T2‑T3, T3‑T1) using a low‑ohm meter; compare to the name‑plate or typical values for this motor type.  \n4. If one winding shows a significantly higher resistance, replace or repair that winding/lead.  \n\n*If the individual winding measurements all come out equal, the high T1‑T3 reading could be due to a poor connection at the terminal block rather than the winding itself.*"}
